WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.820
A tribunal based at The Hague recently
issued an award in an international

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:13.200
arbitration involving the Philippines
and China. First, it found that China's

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.920
claims to historic rights in the South
China Sea have no basis either in

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.650
international law or in historical fact.

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.039
Secondly, it found that none of the
Spratly Islands are, legally speaking,

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:27.689
islands. Rather, they're rocks or parts of
the seabed exposed at low tide. That

00:00:27.689 --> 00:00:31.500
means they don't generate large maritime
zones that states can claim as their own.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:35.370
And finally, it found that China acted
illegally by denying Philippine

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.700
fishermen access to the Scarborough
Shoal, by destroying the marine

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.239
environment in and around the Spratly
Islands, and by building an artificial

00:00:42.239 --> 00:00:45.420
island within the Philippines' exclusive
economic zone.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:50.250
Why does this matter? Well, it severely
curtails the amount of maritime territory

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.829
that China can legally claim in the
South China Sea.

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:56.850
Secondly, it could and should serve as
the basis upon which future negotiations

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.800
over territorial dispersement could
proceed. And finally, how China reacts to

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:06.800
the ruling will clarify whether it's a
country that upholds the rule of law—or not.

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.490
How do you think China will respond to
The Hague tribunal's ruling? Let us know

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.909
in the comments and also let us know
what other topics you want AEI scholars

00:01:14.909 --> 00:01:16.110
to cover in 60 seconds.

